    "lawCode": "Add §207, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8348A\n\nSPONSOR: BROUK\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to establishing a\ncampaign to promote public awareness of the causes of oral disease and\nits prevention\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to raise public awareness about the impor-\ntance of good oral health to good overall health and well-being.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Adds subdivision s of section 207 of the public health law to\nauthorize the department to conduct an education and outreach program\nfor consumers, patients, educators, and health care providers relating\nto the importance of good oral health, including by not limited to, the\nimpact of oral disease, causes of oral disease and its prevention, oral\nhealth across the lifespan, the total body connection, the value of\nearly detection and the availability of oral health services in the\ncommunity.\n\n§ 2 establishes the effective date.\n\n \nDIFFERENCE BETWEEN ORIGINAL AND AND AMENDED VERSION (IF APPLICABLE):\n\nSection 1 makes technical changes.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nGood oral health is essential for vital functions of life, including\neating, breathing and speaking. Dental disease can result \"in poor\nnutrition, school absences, missed workdays and increasing public and\nprivate expenditures for dental care.\" Periodontal disease increases the\nrisk of heart disease, diabetes and premature birth. Dentists are impor-\ntant front-line health care providers in that infectious diseases,\ncancer, immune disorders, and nutritional deficiencies are often\nreflected in the health of a patient's teeth and gums. This bill will\nprovide for a public awareness campaign to underline the importance of\ndental care and oral hygiene in promoting overall health and well-being.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nTBD\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the sixtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n1 Bureau of Dental Health, New York State Department of Health. The\nImpact of Oral Disease in New York State. 2006.\n",
